@c ====================================================================
@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex introduction
@cindex Gas City
@cindex porcelain

Gas City is a system for running fleets of coding agents.  Its
@command{gc} command-line tool is the @dfn{plumbing}: it starts and
stops agents, routes work, and stores issues.  @code{gascity.el} is the
@dfn{porcelain}: a set of sectioned, keyboard-driven Emacs buffers that
render @command{gc}'s state and run its commands, in the deliberate style
of Magit and Forge.

@section Every view is a function of @command{gc} output

@cindex JSON
@cindex data plane
There is one rule behind the whole package: every view is a function of
@code{gc @dots{} --json} output.  A single module runs @command{gc},
decodes its JSON, and hands typed objects to the views; the views render
those objects and bind keys back to @command{gc} commands.  Nothing is
cached behind your back and nothing is guessed --- what you see is what
@command{gc} reports, and @kbd{g} re-reads it.

@section What you get

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{A cockpit} (@pxref{The Cockpit}) --- one short triage buffer
per city: what needs you, what is moving, who is working, what is
queued, what just happened, and the rigs.
@item
@strong{Detail views} (@pxref{The Rig Dashboard}, @pxref{Session
Detail}) --- a single rig or a single agent, with its work, mail and
history.
@item
@strong{Tabulated lists} (@pxref{The Lists}) --- rigs, sessions,
convoys, orders, mail and Dolt databases, each paginated, sortable and
filterable.
@item
@strong{At-point actions} (@pxref{Actions}) --- nudge, suspend, kill,
wake, drain, sling and more, run on the object under point and reported
in the echo area.
@end itemize

@section Two kinds of buffer

@cindex vui
@cindex tabulated-list-mode
The porcelain is hand-built with two rendering technologies, chosen per
view:

@table @asis
@item vui
A small React-like reconciler drives the heterogeneous
@dfn{dashboards} --- the cockpit and the rig and session detail
views --- where sections of different shapes are stacked and refreshed
independently.
@item @code{tabulated-list-mode}
The built-in Emacs facility drives the homogeneous @dfn{lists}, where
every row has the same columns.
@end table

@cindex beads.el
Issue (@dfn{bead}) display itself is delegated to a sibling package,
@code{beads.el}: when you press @key{RET} on a bead, gascity opens it in
beads.el, scoped to the right rig's store.  gascity is the city-level
porcelain; beads.el is the issue-level porcelain.

@c ====================================================================
@node Installation
@chapter Installation
@cindex installation

@section Requirements

@cindex requirements
@cindex dependencies
@table @asis
@item GNU Emacs 29.1 or later
The porcelain uses facilities (e.g.@: @code{tabulated-list} truncation,
modern @code{transient}) that assume a recent Emacs.
@item The @command{gc} CLI on @code{exec-path}
gascity shells out to @command{gc} for every read and every action.  A
bare command name is looked up on @code{exec-path}; set
@code{gascity-executable} to use an absolute path (@pxref{Customization}).
@item @code{beads.el} and @code{vui} on the @code{load-path}
@code{beads.el} provides the @code{beads-meta}, @code{beads-section},
@code{beads-terminal} and @code{beads-pager} features that gascity builds
on; @code{vui} is the dashboard reconciler.
@item A running city (for live data)
With no reachable @command{gc}, views degrade gracefully to an error
screen reading @samp{Press g to retry} rather than signalling.
@end table

@cindex vterm
@cindex eat
Optionally, install @code{vterm} or @code{eat} for a nicer terminal when
attaching to an agent's tmux session; without either, gascity falls back
to the built-in @code{term} (@pxref{Customization}).

The package header declares the full dependency set:

@example
(emacs "29.1") (transient "0.10.1") (vui "1.0.0")
(beads "0.1.0") (sesman "0.3.2")
@end example

@noindent
where @code{beads} is beads.el and @code{sesman} is pulled in
transitively through beads.el's terminal stack.

@section Loading from a checkout

@cindex loading
Put the @file{lisp} directory on your @code{load-path} and require the
entry point, which loads every module:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/gascity.el/lisp")
(require 'gascity)
@end lisp

@noindent
You are ready: run @kbd{M-x gascity-dashboard} (@pxref{Getting Started}).

@c ====================================================================
@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started
@cindex getting started
@cindex tour

@findex gascity-dashboard
The fastest way in is the cockpit.  With a city running, type

@example
M-x gascity-dashboard
@end example

@noindent
to open the city's cockpit: a header line with the city and whether it
refreshes live, two summary lines, then six short sections --- Needs
you, Moving, Agents, Work, Activity and Rigs.

Move from thing to thing with @key{TAB} and @kbd{S-@key{TAB}}, toggle
the thing at point with @key{SPC} (fold a section, expand a fold row,
open a row's inline detail), act on it with @key{RET}, and reload with
@kbd{g}.  @kbd{?} lists every verb under the key it has in the views and
@kbd{j} jumps to another view.  These keys are shared by every gascity
buffer; they are described once in @ref{Buffers and Navigation}.

@section The dispatcher

@findex gascity
@cindex dispatcher
@cindex transient
Every entry point and action is also reachable from one command-dispatch
menu.  Type @kbd{M-x gascity} to raise it:

@shot{dispatch-dark, The command dispatcher (ef-elea-dark)}

@noindent
The left columns open the porcelain views --- @kbd{h} the cockpit, the
@strong{Lists} column the tabulated lists --- and the
@strong{Dispatch} column routes the mutating actions, prompting for their
arguments (@pxref{Actions}).  The same actions are available at point in
the views, so the dispatcher is a backend, not the primary interface.

@cindex entry points
@noindent
The view-opening commands, all autoloaded, are:

@table @code
@item gascity-dashboard
The city cockpit (@pxref{The Cockpit}).
@item gascity-rig-dashboard
A single rig in detail; prompts for the rig (@pxref{The Rig Dashboard}).
@item gascity-rig-list
@itemx gascity-session-list
@itemx gascity-convoy-list
@itemx gascity-mail-inbox
@itemx gascity-order-list
@itemx gascity-dolt-list
The six tabulated lists (@pxref{The Lists}).
@item gascity
The dispatcher transient.
@end table

@c ====================================================================
@node Buffers and Navigation
@chapter Buffers and Navigation
@cindex navigation
@cindex section model

Every gascity buffer is read-only and keyboard-driven.  The dashboards
share a Magit-like @dfn{section model}; the lists share a pagination and
sorting model.  This chapter documents the keys common to all of them;
each later chapter adds only the keys unique to its view.

@section The section model

@cindex sections
@cindex collapsing
The vui dashboards are built from @dfn{sections}: a header line (the city
scope, a rig, an agent group) followed by its rows.  Two granularities of
motion move through them --- by line and by section --- and a section can
be collapsed to its header.

@table @kbd
@item n
@kindex n
@findex next-line
Move down one line.
@item p
@kindex p
@findex previous-line
Move up one line.
@item N
@kindex N
@findex gascity-section-next
Jump to the next section header (the coarse counterpart to @kbd{n}).
@item P
@kindex P
@findex gascity-section-previous
Jump to the previous section header.
@item @key{TAB}
@itemx S-@key{TAB}
@kindex TAB
@kindex S-TAB
@findex gascity-thing-forward
@findex gascity-thing-backward
Move to the next (previous) @dfn{thing}: a section header, a row that
carries an object, a fold row, or a @samp{@dots{} N more} line.
Decoration is skipped; at the end the motion wraps and says so.
@item @key{SPC}
@kindex SPC
@findex gascity-thing-toggle
Toggle the thing at point: fold or unfold a section, expand or collapse
a fold row, open or close a row's inline detail drawer.  Folds and
drawers survive a refresh.
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET
Act on the thing at point --- the precise meaning depends on the view
(attach a terminal, open a bead, open a section's full view).  @key{RET}
never folds.
@item ?
@kindex ?
@findex gascity-dispatch
The dispatch menu: every verb, under the key it has in the views.
@item j
@kindex j
Jump to another view: @kbd{j j} the cockpit, @kbd{j a} agents, @kbd{j b}
beads, @kbd{j m} mail, @kbd{j o}, @kbd{j v}, @kbd{j d} orders, convoys and
Dolt, @kbd{j g} a rig dashboard.
@item /
@kindex /
The view's filter menu; every change applies at once, @kbd{x} resets.
@item q
@kindex q
@findex quit-window
Bury the buffer.
@item g
@kindex g
Reload the view from @command{gc}, preserving point and collapse state.
@end table

@cindex nudge, mnemonic
@cindex peek, mnemonic
@quotation Note
Because @kbd{N} and @kbd{P} take the section-motion keys and @kbd{p}
takes previous-line, the two actions that once lived there moved: agent
@dfn{nudge} is now @kbd{M} (``message'') and @dfn{peek} is now @kbd{v}.
@end quotation

@section Objects at point

@cindex object at point
Actions resolve the @dfn{object at point} through one precedence ladder:
an agent, then a bead id, then the current list row, then the buffer's
subject (the agent a detail view is @emph{about}).  This is why an action
key works whether point is on a specific row or anywhere in a detail view
that has a single subject.

@section Lists: pagination and sorting

@cindex pagination
@cindex sorting
The tabulated lists size each page to the window and carry these keys in
addition to the standard @code{tabulated-list-mode} bindings (column
sorting on the header line, and so on):

@table @kbd
@item ]
@kindex ]
@findex gascity-tabulated-next-page
Next page.
@item [
@kindex [
@findex gascity-tabulated-prev-page
Previous page.
@item G
@kindex G
@findex gascity-tabulated-goto-page
Jump to a page number (prompts).
@item S
@kindex S
Sling (the same in every view); sort with a header click or @kbd{/ -S}
instead, which sorts across @emph{all} pages.
@item @key{SPC}
@kindex SPC @r{(lists)}
@findex gascity-tabulated-detail-toggle
Show the row at point in a @file{*gascity-detail*} side window, or close
it.  @kbd{C-o} shows it without selecting it and @kbd{C-c C-f} makes it
follow point; @kbd{q} closes it before burying the list.
@item /
@kindex /
Open the list's filter menu: changes apply at once, @kbd{x} resets,
@kbd{-S} sorts.
@end table

@noindent
The mode line shows @samp{Name [page/total] (filter)}; an over-long cell
is truncated to its column with an ellipsis and a @code{help-echo}
tooltip carrying the full value.

@c ====================================================================
@node The Cockpit
@chapter The Cockpit
@cindex cockpit
@findex gascity-dashboard

@kbd{M-x gascity-dashboard} opens the city's cockpit in
@code{gascity-dashboard-mode}: one short triage buffer answering, top to
bottom, what needs you, what is moving, who is working, what is queued
and what just happened.  No section shows more than
@code{gascity-dashboard-section-rows} rows (five); the rest is a
@samp{@dots{} N more} line whose @key{RET} opens the full view.

@section What it shows

The header line names the city (with @samp{@@host} for a remote one),
whether it refreshes live (@kbd{W}) and how long ago it last did.  The
buffer opens with the city and its path, then one summary line: agents
running out of total, live sessions, active and failed runs, ready
beads, unread mail and the beads store's size (@samp{◐} when
@command{gc} reports partial errors, with the reason as a tooltip).

@table @asis
@item Needs you
Stalled agents (running without a live session), sessions that crashed
or timed out and were not woken since, runs with no step progress for
@code{gascity-dashboard-run-idle-threshold} seconds, runs that failed,
beads reopened after their assignee died, beads labelled
@samp{gc:escalation} or @samp{hold:*}, unread mail and store-health
warnings --- @samp{■} before @samp{▲}.
@item Moving
Active runs with their step ladder (@samp{◆} done, @samp{⬣} active,
@samp{·} pending, @samp{✕} failed) and their live workers; in-progress
work outside a run is listed flat.
@item Agents
Running agents by recent activity, then idle ones; stopped agents fold
into one @samp{▸ stopped} line.  Named sessions such as the mayor are
agents too.
@item Work
Ready beads by priority, then age.  Wisps, nudge beads, order churn,
message beads and session beads are hidden, always with a
@samp{(N hidden)} count.
@item Activity
Recent events (@code{gascity-dashboard-window}, two hours by default).
Order firings, noise-bead lifecycle and bead updates fold into
@samp{×N} rows per 15 minutes; signal events never fold.
@item Rigs
One row per rig: prefix, path, agents running, work in progress.
@end table

A section still loading shows @samp{@dots{}}; one whose read failed
shows @samp{■ gc @dots{}: @var{reason}   g retry}; one that failed over
good data keeps its rows and marks its header @samp{◐}; an empty one
reads @samp{none}.

@section Keys

Besides the shared keys (@pxref{Buffers and Navigation}):

@table @kbd
@item /
@kindex / @r{(cockpit)}
@findex gascity-dashboard-filter
The filter: @kbd{-w}, @kbd{-n}, @kbd{-o}, @kbd{-m} show wisps, nudge
beads, order churn and message beads; @kbd{-r} narrows to a rig;
@kbd{-W} sets the Activity window; @kbd{-c} unfolds churn.  Filters are
remembered per city for the session.
@item W
@kindex W @r{(cockpit)}
@findex gascity-dashboard-toggle-live
Toggle live refresh (@code{gascity-dashboard-live-interval}).
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET @r{(cockpit)}
@findex gascity-dashboard-activate
On a section header, open the section's view; on an agent, attach its
terminal; on a run, open its detail; on a bead, open it in beads.el; on
a rig, open its dashboard.
@item i
@itemx v
@itemx d
@itemx t
Agent detail, peek, Dired and tmux on an agent row.
@item M
@itemx s
@itemx K
@itemx w
@itemx D
@itemx R
@itemx U
Nudge, suspend, kill, wake, drain, reset and undrain the agent at point;
on a rig row @kbd{s}, @kbd{r} and @kbd{R} suspend, resume and restart
the rig.
@item l
@kindex l @r{(cockpit)}
@findex gascity-dashboard-rig-log
The git log of the rig at point.
@item S
@itemx c
@itemx m
Sling, bead actions and mail actions.
@end table

@c ====================================================================
@node The Rig Dashboard
@chapter The Rig Dashboard
@cindex rig dashboard
@findex gascity-rig-dashboard

@kbd{M-x gascity-rig-dashboard} prompts for a rig (defaulting to the
contextual one) and opens @file{*gascity-rig:@: NAME*} in
@code{gascity-rig-dashboard-mode}.  It is the single-rig counterpart to
the cockpit, with five independently-loaded sections so that a slow
or failing one never blanks the rest.

@shot{rig-dashboard-dark, The rig dashboard (ef-elea-dark)}

@section What it shows

@table @asis
@item Header
Rig name, bead-id prefix, branch, suspended or running, bead-store
status, and the owning city.
@item Agents
The rig's agents as @code{● qualified-name state short-name}, joined to
@code{gc session list} so the action keys can act on them.
@item Ready
The rig's ready beads (@code{gc bd ready}); @key{RET} opens one in
beads.el.
@item In progress
The rig's in-progress beads.
@item Orders
The rig-scoped orders.
@item Dolt
The rig's Dolt commit count.
@end table

@section Keys

The rig dashboard shares the cockpit's agent keys (@kbd{i}, @kbd{d},
@kbd{t}, @kbd{M}, @kbd{s}, @kbd{K}, @kbd{w}, @kbd{D}) and adds @kbd{l},
the rig repository's git log, and:

@table @kbd
@item g
@kindex g @r{(rig dashboard)}
@findex gascity-rig-dashboard-refresh
Reload, preserving point.
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET @r{(rig dashboard)}
@findex gascity-rig-dashboard-activate
On a bead, open it in beads.el; on an agent, attach its terminal.
@item b
@kindex b @r{(rig dashboard)}
@findex gascity-rig-dashboard-beads
Open the @emph{whole rig's} bead board (acts on the dashboard's rig, not
point).
@item v
@kindex v @r{(rig dashboard)}
@findex gascity-session-peek-at-point
Peek at the agent's recent output (@pxref{Actions}).
@end table

@cindex HQ
@quotation Note
The city HQ appears in the rig list (it owns beads) but is not a
configured rig and has no dashboard; @key{RET} on it points you to
@code{gascity-dashboard} instead.
@end quotation

@c ====================================================================
@node Session Detail
@chapter Session Detail
@cindex session detail
@cindex polecat
@findex gascity-polecat-detail

Press @kbd{i} on an agent (in the cockpit, the rig dashboard, or the
session list) to open its detail view, @file{*gascity-agent:@: NAME*}, in
@code{gascity-session-detail-mode}.

@shot{session-detail-light, A single agent in detail (ef-cyprus)}

@section What it shows

@table @asis
@item Agent
A state block: state, provider, attached, last-active, worktree, and tmux
target.
@item mail
The agent's inbox message count.
@item On hook
The in-progress bead(s) the agent is working --- or @samp{idle --- no
work on hook}.
@item Recent history
The agent's other recent beads, newest first (capped at ten).
@end table

@cindex work_dir
@noindent
The history is gathered from three reads --- by the runtime session name,
by the qualified agent name, and by @code{metadata.work_dir} --- so that
work a finished agent's beads were reassigned away from is still
recovered and shown.

@section Keys

The detail view shares the session action keys and adds:

@table @kbd
@item g
@kindex g @r{(session detail)}
@findex gascity-polecat-detail-refresh
Reload, preserving point.
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET @r{(session detail)}
@findex gascity-session-detail-activate
Open the bead at point in beads.el.
@item v
@kindex v @r{(session detail)}
@findex gascity-session-peek-at-point
Peek at the agent's recent output.
@end table

@noindent
Because the buffer has a single subject agent, the action keys (@kbd{M},
@kbd{s}, @kbd{K}, @kbd{w}, @kbd{D}, @kbd{d}, @kbd{t}) act on that agent
wherever point happens to be.

