@c ====================================================================
@node Actions
@chapter Actions
@cindex actions
@cindex mutating commands

Beyond reading, gascity dispatches @command{gc}'s mutating commands.
Every action is reachable two ways: @dfn{at point} (a key acts on the rig,
agent or order under point, then refreshes the view) and @dfn{by prompt}
(from the dispatcher's sub-transients or @kbd{M-x}, reading arguments with
completion over live @command{gc} data).  Quick mutations run
synchronously and report a one-line outcome in the echo area; a failure
is re-signalled as a clean @code{user-error} carrying @command{gc}'s
standard error.

@section Session actions

@cindex nudge
@cindex suspend
@cindex kill
@cindex drain
@cindex peek
Run at point in the dashboards and the session list, or by prompt from
@kbd{A} in the dispatcher (@code{gascity-session-dispatch}):

@table @asis
@item Nudge --- @kbd{M}
@code{gc session nudge}: send the agent a message (prompts for it).  The
ephemeral, zero-overhead way to signal an agent.
@item Suspend --- @kbd{s}
@code{gc session suspend}: stop the agent's runtime; its bead persists.
@item Kill --- @kbd{K}
@code{gc session kill}: force-kill the runtime (the reconciler may
restart it).  Confirms first.
@item Wake --- @kbd{w}
@code{gc session wake}: wake a suspended agent.
@item Drain --- @kbd{D}
@code{gc runtime drain}: wind the agent down gracefully --- it finishes
its current task, then exits.  Distinct from suspend and kill; cancel with
@code{gc runtime undrain}.
@item Peek --- @kbd{v}
@code{gc session peek}: show the agent's recent terminal output in a
read-only buffer.
@end table

@section Rig actions

By prompt from @kbd{R} in the dispatcher (@code{gascity-rig-dispatch}), or
at point in the rig list:

@table @asis
@item Suspend --- @kbd{s}
@code{gc rig suspend}.
@item Resume --- @kbd{r}
@code{gc rig resume}.
@item Restart --- @kbd{R}
@code{gc rig restart}.  Confirms, as it kills the rig's agent sessions.
@end table

@section Dispatch and lifecycle

@cindex sling
@cindex order
@cindex city lifecycle
@table @asis
@item Sling --- dispatcher @kbd{S}
@findex gascity-sling
@code{gc sling}: route a bead id, a formula, or free task text to an
agent.  Supports a formula, a nudge, and a dry run.
@item Run order --- dispatcher @kbd{O}, list @kbd{x}
@findex gascity-order-run
@code{gc order run}: run an order now, bypassing its trigger.
@item City lifecycle --- dispatcher @kbd{L}
@findex gascity-start
@findex gascity-stop
@code{gc start} and @code{gc stop} start and stop the whole city.  Both
confirm first and stream their (long-running) output; a prefix argument to
stop forces a hard, graceless shutdown.
@end table

@section Attached terminals

@cindex attach
@cindex scrolling a terminal
@cindex copy-mode
@kbd{t} on an agent (@code{gascity-tmux-at-point}) opens a terminal buffer
holding a live @command{tmux} client.  A tmux client draws on the
@dfn{alternate screen}, so the Emacs buffer contains only what is on
screen right now: the history is tmux's, and the backend's own scrollback
and copy commands have nothing to show.  Scrolling therefore goes through
tmux:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-b [}
Enter tmux's copy-mode.  @kbd{C-b} is the prefix on Gas City's per-city
tmux servers; a personal @file{~/.tmux.conf} elsewhere may rebind it.
@item @key{PageUp} / @key{PageDown}
Page through the history once in copy-mode --- and @kbd{C-b @key{PageUp}}
enters copy-mode a page back in one go.  @kbd{M-v} and @kbd{C-v} do the
same, and the arrow keys move a line at a time.
@item @kbd{q}
Leave copy-mode and return to the live pane.
@end table

@cindex bead at point, terminal
@findex gascity-bead-show-at-point
Agent transcripts are full of bead ids.  Put point on one --- the
backend's own copy mode, @kbd{C-c C-t} in vterm, frees point from the
terminal cursor --- and Eldoc shows the bead's status and title
(beads.el's @code{beads-eldoc-mode}, looked up asynchronously and scoped
to the rig owning the id's prefix), while @kbd{C-c b}
(@code{gascity-bead-show-at-point}) opens it in beads.el's detail view.
The same command works in any text buffer, a shell or a mail body
included.

These keys only reach tmux because gascity suppresses the global minor
modes named in @code{gascity-terminal-unshadow-minor-modes} inside its
terminal buffers; a minor-mode map outranks the terminal backend's own
keymap, so without that, @code{pixel-scroll-precision-mode} (for
instance) swallows @key{PageUp} and @key{PageDown} and scrolls the Emacs
window --- which has nothing above the visible screen to scroll to.

The mouse wheel is a tmux setting, not an Emacs one: it scrolls the
history only where the city's tmux server runs with @code{mouse on}
(@code{set -g mouse on}), which is not the default.  How far back you can
scroll is tmux's @code{history-limit} --- 2000 lines unless the city says
otherwise.

